{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAG4RRtS7RyjtoH3JrsC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I changed `Hijack()` so it now fails only once body bytes have been written, not as soon as headers go out. Before, any call after the headers were sent, such as the 101 response websocket libraries send, returned `errHijackAlreadyWritten`. Hijacking before anything is written, or after headers only, now succeeds.\n\n- **Changed:**\n  - `response_writer.go`: the check is now `w.size > 0` instead of `w.Written()`, with a comment explaining why.\n  - `response_writer_test.go`: added a case that sets status 101, sends the headers, then hijacks.\n- **Why this and not something bigger:** it's a one-line condition change. The rest of the lifecycle is unchanged: hijacking still marks the response as written, and hijacking after a body write still returns the error.\n- **Verified:**\n  - The new case failed before the fix (\"unexpected error from Hijack()\") and passes after it.\n  - All three hijack cases and `TestResponseWriterHijack` pass.\n  - `go test ./...` passes in every package, and `go vet` and `gofmt -l` report nothing.\n- **Not done:** nothing is committed, and the repo is on a detached HEAD. Tell me if you want it committed on a branch as `fix(response): refine hijack behavior for response lifecycle`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":997,"cache_read_input_tokens":38993,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":997,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1ff06f4e-d799-4742-8ce2-f64784d50493","uuid":"551320d8-3dc2-49a7-b452-b83cca583ef8","timestamp":"2026-10-18T19:32:49.442Z","request_id":"req_011CgAG4RCVt6r8CgTVv1LEG"}
